> > > > There are other ways you could use for assessing overdispersion in
> > > > the context of MSOMs using the 'occuMulti' function. For instance,
> > > > you could use the Deviance-Based Goodness-of-Fit (GOF)
> > > > Test. It basically relies on a parametric bootstrap that can be
> > > > used to simulate new datasets under the fitted model, and compare
> > > > observed deviance with the simulated distribution. If the observed
> > > > deviance is much higher than the simulated values, overdispersion
> > > > is likely.
> > > >
> > > > Anyway, with regards to the Chi-Square Goodness-of-Fit Test,
> > > > overdispersion is a serious problem if the variance inflation
> > > > factor ĉ is much higher than 1.5. In your case, the value is very
> > > > close to 1.5. So, I don't think it represents a serious problem. If
> > > > the model performs well (e.g., no strong residual patterns), you
> > > > might not need major adjustments.
